<p>The live index can change independently of this page. Use <code>browse_packs</code> to fetch the configured repository's current <code>index.json</code>.</p>

<h2 id="browse">browse_packs</h2>
<p>This network tool accepts an optional <code>query</code> string and fetches:</p>
<div class="code-block">https://raw.githubusercontent.com/&lt;skillsRepo&gt;/main/index.json</div>
<p>It filters on name, description, or category and returns <code>total</code> plus pack objects containing <code>name</code>, <code>description</code>, <code>categories</code>, and a textual install example. The handler currently drops the <code>skills</code> count present in the index.</p>

<h2 id="manifest">pack.json schema</h2>
<p>Current registry manifests use:</p>
<div class="code-block">{
  <span class="kw">"name"</span>: <span class="str">"api-design"</span>,
  <span class="kw">"version"</span>: <span class="str">"1.0.0"</span>,
  <span class="kw">"description"</span>: <span class="str">"REST API conventions ..."</span>,
  <span class="kw">"skills"</span>: [<span class="str">"api-design.md"</span>]
}</div>
<p>The runtime installer requires only a non-empty <code>skills</code> array before fetching its entries. The community repository's CI is stricter: it checks pack name/directory agreement, version and description strings, listed/orphan Markdown files, basic skill frontmatter, a <code>##</code> section, index consistency, and the vendored content-guard patterns.</p>
<p>CI is structural and pattern-based. It is not a security audit, factual review, or provenance verification.</p>

<h2 id="install">Install by registry name</h2>
<div class="code-block">install_pack({ <span class="kw">pack</span>: <span class="str">"api-design"</span> })</div>
<p>The default downloader fetches <code>packs/api-design/pack.json</code>, then each filename listed there, from the configured GitHub repository.</p>

<h2 id="direct-url">Install from a direct URL</h2>
<p><code>url</code> is a separate argument and <code>pack</code> remains required:</p>
<div class="code-block">install_pack({
  <span class="kw">pack</span>: <span class="str">"team-patterns"</span>,
  <span class="kw">url</span>: <span class="str">"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/example/team-skills/main/packs/team-patterns/pack.json"</span>
})</div>
<p>By default, a direct URL must use HTTPS and the exact host <code>raw.githubusercontent.com</code>. Relative skill filenames are fetched beside that <code>pack.json</code>. It is a JSON-and-files protocol; tarballs, GitHub release archives, local paths, and export directories are not accepted.</p>
<p><code>allowArbitraryPackUrls: true</code> in <code>config.json</code> bypasses the direct-host allowlist. The pack downloader is separate from the research SSRF-guarded fetcher: it has time and size caps but does not perform the same private-address resolution, IP pinning, or redirect re-vetting. Enable arbitrary URLs only when you understand that difference.</p>
<p><code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> is attached only when the request hostname is <code>raw.githubusercontent.com</code> or <code>api.github.com</code>; other hosts receive no token.</p>

<h2 id="install-semantics">What an install actually does</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Fetches and parses <code>pack.json</code>.</li>
  <li>Fetches every listed skill before starting writes. A fetch error stops the call before writes.</li>
  <li>Runs the content guard on each fetched file.</li>
  <li>Skips an exact content-hash duplicate.</li>
  <li>Treats different content with the same parsed skill name as an update.</li>
  <li>Writes accepted install/update files, removes replaced filenames when they differ, optionally re-signs, and rebuilds the store.</li>
</ol>

<div class="warning-box"><strong>Partial acceptance is intentional in current code.</strong> A content-guard match adds that file to <code>rejected</code>; it does not reject the whole pack. Other files can still install.</div>

<div class="warning-box"><strong>Same-name updates can overwrite local work.</strong> Deduplication does not preserve every local modification. Exact content is skipped, but a different file with the same frontmatter <code>name</code> is an update regardless of its version.</div>

<p>The write loop attempts a best-effort rollback when a write throws, but it does not snapshot prior same-filename content. Do not describe the operation as fully transactional or rely on it as a backup strategy.</p>

<h2 id="result">Install result</h2>
<div class="code-block">{
  <span class="kw">"installed"</span>: <span class="val">1</span>,
  <span class="kw">"updated"</span>: <span class="val">0</span>,
  <span class="kw">"skipped"</span>: <span class="val">0</span>,
  <span class="kw">"rejected"</span>: <span class="val">0</span>,
  <span class="kw">"details"</span>: {
    <span class="kw">"installed"</span>: [<span class="str">"api-design.md"</span>],
    <span class="kw">"updated"</span>: [],
    <span class="kw">"skipped"</span>: [],
    <span class="kw">"rejected"</span>: []
  }
}</div>
<p>Rejected detail entries contain <code>filename</code> and a regex-derived <code>reason</code>. The response does not contain an <code>index_rebuilt</code> flag, pack name, or signing result.</p>

<h2 id="signing">Signing after install</h2>
<p>If accepted files changed the library and <code>keys/private.pem</code> exists, the installer signs <strong>all flat skills</strong> and then rebuilds. Without the key, the files remain unsigned. Because all files are re-signed, unrelated direct edits present at that moment can be incorporated into the new manifest. The result does not tell you whether signing occurred.</p>

<h2 id="flat-layout">Installed layout</h2>
<p>Installed skills are written directly into <code>&lt;home&gt;/skills/</code>:</p>
<div class="code-block"><span class="dim">~/.mcp-librarian/skills/</span>
<span class="dim">  api-design.md</span>
<span class="dim">  security-hardening.md</span></div>
<p>Pack-name subdirectories are not part of the current store layout and would be ignored by its non-recursive loader.</p>

<h2 id="export">Exporting a pack</h2>
<div class="code-block">export_pack({
  <span class="kw">name</span>: <span class="str">"team-patterns"</span>,
  <span class="kw">description</span>: <span class="str">"Team runbooks"</span>,
  <span class="kw">skills</span>: [<span class="str">"deployment"</span>, <span class="str">"incident-response"</span>]
})</div>
<p>This writes <code>&lt;home&gt;/exports/team-patterns/pack.json</code> plus flat Markdown copies and returns <code>{ exported, path, pack }</code>. <code>install_pack</code> cannot import that directory directly; publish it at the expected HTTP layout or copy reviewed files into <code>skills/</code> yourself.</p>
